# Line endings are a property of THIS REPOSITORY, not of each developer's git
# configuration.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# Without it, checkout depends on the machine's global `core.autocrlf`. On the
# Windows PC this project moved to, `core.autocrlf=true`, so every tracked text
# file arrived in the working tree with CRLF while the committed blobs are LF.
# Two concrete consequences were measured:
#
#   1. `npm run format:check` failed on 37 files — Prettier's default
#      `endOfLine: "lf"` treats a CRLF file as unformatted. That broke
#      `npm run verify`, the project's own gate, on a clean checkout.
#   2. The pipeline-owned artifacts in `web/src/data/generated/` no longer
#      hashed to the values recorded in KIRO.md §9: `panel.json` hashed to
#      `6e03f7a8…` on disk instead of `e446aeb5…`. LF-normalising the same bytes
#      reproduced the recorded digest exactly, so the artifacts were never
#      modified — only the checkout was. But an analytical hash that cannot be
#      verified on a given machine is a reproducibility claim that machine
#      cannot check.
#
# `python -m pipeline.build --check` was verified to pass either way: it reads in
# Python text mode, so it is newline-insensitive. The freshness gate was never
# affected — only the byte-level digests above and the formatting gate.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the working tree to LF on every platform regardless of
# `core.autocrlf`, which is what makes those checks mean the same thing on
# Windows, Linux and macOS.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
